    explanation_en: "- **Option B is CORRECT** because an Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling group with a mixed instances policy allows defining a guaranteed base capacity of On-Demand Instances (fulfilling the requirement of at least 4 baseline instances) while fulfilling all additional dynamic scaling needs with Spot Instances across multiple instance types. This achieves up to 90% cost savings on surge compute while eliminating the risk of losing baseline capacity.\n- **Option D is CORRECT** because EC2 Spot Instances can be reclaimed with a 2-minute warning. By capturing Spot Instance Interruption Notices via Amazon EventBridge, the worker application can stop processing cleanly. Because the jobs are tracked via Amazon SQS, failing to delete an in-progress message causes the SQS visibility timeout to expire, automatically returning the task to the queue for another instance to process without data loss.\n- **Option A is INCORRECT** because using 100% Spot Instances cannot guarantee the minimum baseline throughput of 4 instances if AWS reclaims capacity in that pool. Furthermore, deploying into a single Availability Zone creates a single point of failure and increases the likelihood of simultaneous interruptions.\n- **Option C is INCORRECT** because Dedicated Hosts provide physical server isolation for compliance and bring-your-own-license (BYOL) requirements, but they are significantly more expensive and wasteful for handling unpredictable peak workloads, directly violating the requirement for the most cost-effective solution."
